N={
"hiruta25_interspeech":("An ASR training set should cover acoustic diversity and should spend labeling effort where the transcription is uncertain.","Random sampling can overrepresent easy or similar speech, while uncertainty-only sampling can repeat rare errors without broad acoustic coverage.","Sample randomly, or rank clips by model uncertainty alone and call the resulting set diverse.","Combine acoustic-diversity measures with transcription uncertainty when selecting training data, then test the effect on ASR.","The paper proposes hybrid data sampling for ASR by integrating acoustic diversity and transcription uncertainty.","Data selection is a coverage-allocation problem: diversity broadens the conditions seen, while uncertainty targets the model's unresolved boundary.","The paper reports ASR improvements from hybrid sampling relative to the tested selection strategies.","Acoustic representation, uncertainty estimator, corpus, budget, language, and split bound the result; a sampling score is not a complete measure of data value."),
"hu25f_interspeech":("A speech-to-speech language model in a duplex conversation must listen and speak at overlapping times without losing the current turn or waiting for a full utterance.","Duplex interaction couples streaming recognition, generation, interruption, and turn timing; a model optimized for one direction can block or talk over the other.","Use a turn-taking pipeline that waits for end-of-speech or run separate listen and speak models with no shared state.","Build an efficient direct duplex model that represents incoming and outgoing speech jointly and evaluates real-time speech-to-speech behavior.","The paper studies efficient and direct duplex modeling for speech-to-speech language models.","Duplexity is a control problem over concurrent streams: the model must decide what to retain, when to respond, and when to yield while generating speech.","The paper reports efficiency and interactive speech-to-speech results for the duplex model.","Latency, overlap, interruptions, model size, dialogue tasks, and evaluation protocol bound the result; a real-time demo is not robust open-ended conversation."),
"huang25e_interspeech":("A speech deepfake detector should generalize to unseen generators and conditions rather than becoming sharp only on the training attacks.","Sharp decision boundaries can fit artifacts specific to known generators; improving training fit may reduce performance when the attack distribution changes.","Optimize closed-set accuracy and assume a sharper classifier is more discriminative under every attack.","Study how sharpness relates to deepfake-detector generalization and use training changes that improve the boundary's transfer to unseen attacks.","The paper studies moving from sharpness to better generalization for speech deepfake detection.","Generalization depends on the geometry of the learned boundary, not just its training margin; the relevant test is behavior on new attack sources.","The paper reports deepfake-detection generalization results linked to sharpness and the proposed training approach.","Attack types, datasets, sharpness measure, training recipe, and open-set split bound the claim; no benchmark proves future attack coverage."),
"huang25f_interspeech":("Speakers with different articulatory habits can produce similar apical vowels, but the physical movements behind those sounds may differ across Southwestern Mandarin speakers.","Tongue shape, place, and coarticulation interact, and acoustic similarity hides multiple articulatory solutions.","Describe the vowel only by formants or assume one acoustic target has one tongue configuration.","Measure articulatory variation in apical vowels and relate it to acoustic outcomes across Southwestern Mandarin speakers.","The paper analyzes articulatory variations in apical vowels in Southwestern Mandarin.","The sound-to-movement mapping is underdetermined: production data reveal which physical differences are tolerated while the acoustic category stays recognizable.","The paper reports articulatory variation and its acoustic relationships for the studied apical vowels.","Speakers, dialect region, imaging or measurement method, vowel context, and sample size limit generalization; variation is not pathology."),
"liu25c_interspeech":("When editing text-based speech, changing a word should preserve the intended emotion and keep the new audio emotionally consistent with its context.","Text edits can alter sentiment, emphasis, and prosody at once; a locally fluent replacement may sound emotionally wrong next to surrounding speech.","Replace the text and synthesize the whole sentence, or optimize word correctness without an emotion-consistency check.","Create an emotion-consistent speech-editing task and model that preserves affect across the edited span and its context.","EmoCorrector and the ECD-TSE dataset target emotionally consistent text-based speech editing.","Editing has two invariants: linguistic content must change as requested, while speaker identity and emotional trajectory must remain coherent around the change.","The paper reports dataset and model results for emotionally consistent speech editing.","Emotion labels, edit types, speakers, context, synthesis model, and perceptual evaluation bound the claim; emotional consistency is listener- and culture-dependent."),
"liu25d_interspeech":("A bandwidth-limited recording can sound muffled; a bandwidth-extension model should restore useful high-frequency detail without inventing harsh or speaker-inconsistent content.","The missing band is not uniquely determined by the low band, and waveform metrics may reward artifacts that listeners dislike.","Copy the low-band waveform or add fixed high-frequency noise and assume the result is natural.","Use a hybrid waveform bandwidth-extension network and evaluate reconstruction quality, speech content, and perceptual naturalness.","HWB-Net is a high-performance efficient hybrid waveform bandwidth-extension method.","Bandwidth extension is constrained synthesis: the model predicts plausible missing detail from the observed signal while preserving timing and identity in the known band.","The paper reports quality and efficiency results for HWB-Net on bandwidth-extension tests.","Bandwidth limit, speakers, noise, training targets, metrics, and listening protocol bound the result; plausible detail is not recovered ground truth."),
"liu25o_interspeech":("A multilingual speech synthesizer needs token units that can be shared across languages while still preserving language-specific pronunciation and rhythm.","Separate vocabularies waste capacity, while a universal token can erase distinctions or make the language model confuse languages.","Train one token set per language or force all languages into a text-like universal code without testing interference.","Learn a language-independent speech token and use it with language-model synthesis across multiple languages.","LIST is a language-independent speech token for multilingual speech synthesis with language models.","The token is a cross-language interface: shared units carry reusable speech structure, while language conditioning reconstructs the differences needed for intelligible output.","The paper reports multilingual synthesis quality and cross-language token behavior for LIST.","Languages, token rate, codebook, speakers, conditioning, and evaluation metrics bound the claim; shared tokens do not guarantee equal quality."),
"patapati25_interspeech":("An embodied conversational agent should adapt its spoken response to what a person says, sees, and does while the interaction is happening.","Multimodal inputs arrive at different times and the agent must keep a coherent conversational state while meeting real-time limits.","Fuse all sensors once per turn or use a scripted avatar with fixed responses and call it adaptive.","Build a general-purpose real-time multimodal embodied-agent framework that updates perception, dialogue, action, and speech together.","GenECA is a framework for real-time adaptive multimodal embodied conversational agents.","Embodied conversation is a closed loop: perception changes the state, the state selects language and action, and the agent's output changes the next observation.","The paper reports real-time adaptive-agent behavior across its tested multimodal interaction settings.","Sensors, embodiment, latency, dialogue tasks, user studies, and policy constraints bound the claim; a framework demonstration is not human-level social understanding."),
}
